WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.632
A LEFT JOIN is one of the most useful patterns in analytics because it allows us to preserve a clearly defined

00:00:07.632 --> 00:00:10.527
population. Here, patients is on the left.

00:00:10.527 --> 00:00:15.742
That means every patient should remain, whether or not an admission exists.

00:00:15.742 --> 00:00:20.727
Run the query and look for a patient whose admission_id is NULL.

00:00:20.727 --> 00:00:23.637
That NULL does not mean SQL failed.

00:00:23.637 --> 00:00:26.622
It means the patient had no matching admission.

00:00:26.622 --> 00:00:31.602
In fact, that NULL may be exactly what the analyst wanted to find.

00:00:31.602 --> 00:00:35.067
Now imagine a university retention project.

00:00:35.067 --> 00:00:37.972
One table contains every enrolled student.

00:00:37.972 --> 00:00:42.397
Another contains advising appointments. If we use INNER JOIN, students with no appointment disappear.

00:00:42.397 --> 00:00:49.012
But those students may actually be the ones we most want to identify.

00:00:49.012 --> 00:00:54.942
Using LEFT JOIN keeps the full student population and makes the missing appointment visible.

00:00:54.942 --> 00:00:57.447
This is why preservation matters.

00:00:57.447 --> 00:01:02.647
LEFT JOIN is often the right choice when the denominator is all patients, all students, all customers,

00:01:02.647 --> 00:01:05.252
or all products.

00:01:05.252 --> 00:01:12.296
If you use INNER JOIN by mistake, inactive entities disappear and your rates or averages may become biased.
